# SigilGuard Cheatsheet

## Gate Verdicts

SigilGuard gates return `%SigilGuard.Decision{}`. Treat `action` as the 1.0
verdict.

```elixir
alias SigilGuard.ToolGateway

context = [
  phase: :tool_request,
  origin: :model,
  sink: :tool,
  tool: "repo_file_write",
  actor: "spiffe://example.test/agents/release-bot",
  trust_zone: :trusted
]

decision = ToolGateway.guard_request(request, context)

case decision.action do
  :allow -> execute_tool(request)
  :redact -> reject_request(decision)
  :confirm -> request_human_approval(decision)
  :quarantine -> quarantine_request(decision)
  :block -> reject_request(decision)
end
```

Use `SigilGuard.ToolGateway` as the enforcement core. Use
`SigilGuard.MCP.Gateway` only at MCP transport boundaries where
JSON-RPC-compatible helper names and tuple responses are useful.

Common meanings:

- `:allow` — pass the payload through unchanged.
- `:redact` — sensitive content was found. `decision.sanitized_text` is the
  redacted scan text. For a structured request it is a flattened projection,
  not a replacement argument map, so refuse the call unless the host applies
  and validates its own transformation.
- `:confirm` — require an action-bound confirmation token before execution.
- `:quarantine` — isolate the payload and surface evidence to the operator.
- `:block` — deny the operation.

For tool results, flip the boundary direction:

```elixir
result_context = [
  phase: :tool_result,
  origin: :tool,
  sink: :model,
  tool: "repo_file_write",
  actor: "spiffe://example.test/agents/release-bot"
]

decision = ToolGateway.guard_result(result, result_context)
```

## Policy Grammar

Repository policy files are loaded from the <code>SIGILGUARD</code> filename
family:

- `SIGILGUARD_POLICY`
- `.sigilguard-policy`
- `.sigilguard/policy`
- `.github/sigilguard-policy`

Legacy <code>SIGIL</code> filenames fail closed.

Minimal boundary-policy file:

```text
version 3

[rules]
default allow
block phase:tool_request zone:untrusted
confirm sensitivity:private origin:tool,resource,repo sink:external,network
allow phase:tool_result isolation:container,vm,remote_attested effect:read

[repo]
default require_approval
allow agent:release-bot action:modify docs/** CHANGELOG.md
block agent:* priv/secrets/**

[contracts]
contract sink:external,network max_size:16384
  no_raw_credentials:true digest_only_pii:true classes:text
  credential_transform:hash
```

Boundary-rule decisions are `allow`, `redact`, `confirm`, `quarantine`, or
`block`. Matchers are AND-ed by field and comma-separated within a field. Common
matchers include `phase`, `origin`, `sink`, `zone`, `trust`, `sensitivity`,
`isolation`, `effect`, `hits`, and `indicator`.

Repo-policy decisions inside `[repo]` are `allow`, `require_approval`, or
`block`. The runtime gate applies a compiled repo policy passed as
`repo_policy:` at phase `:repo_change`, where `require_approval` becomes the
1.0 `:confirm` verdict.

`SigilGuard.BoundaryPolicy.File.load/1` reads this file. Its `repo` field is
the compiled `[repo]` section, which `SigilGuard.RepoPolicy.evaluate/2` checks
against an agent, an action, and the changed paths:

```elixir
{:ok, policy_file} = SigilGuard.BoundaryPolicy.File.load(repo_root)

decision =
  SigilGuard.RepoPolicy.evaluate(policy_file.repo,
    agent: "release-bot",
    action: "modify",
    changed_paths: ["docs/guide.md", "CHANGELOG.md"]
  )

:allow = decision.verdict
```

A path no rule matches, such as `lib/app.ex`, takes the `[repo]` default and
returns `:require_approval`. `SigilGuard.RepoPolicy.load/1` reads a file that
holds only the flat repo grammar, with no `version 3` line and no section
headers; on the file above it returns `{:error, {:invalid_decision, 0}}`.

Boundary policy can be evaluated directly when the host already has a normalized
boundary. The boundary needs `phase`, `source`, `sink`, and the three SHA-256
digests. A boundary that fails validation is blocked; without the digests the
reason is `invalid_boundary`. `actor` is a map with an `"id"`:

```elixir
{:ok, digests} =
  SigilGuard.Attestation.Digest.digests(:tool_request, request, context)

decision =
  SigilGuard.BoundaryPolicy.evaluate(
    [
      phase: :tool_request,
      source: :model,
      sink: :tool,
      trust_zone: :trusted,
      trust_level: :high,
      actor: %{"id" => "spiffe://example.test/agents/release-bot"},
      action_digest: digests["action"],
      payload_digest: digests["payload"],
      context_digest: digests["context"]
    ],
    policy: policy_file
  )

:allow = decision.action
```

No rule in the file above matches this boundary, so the file's `default allow`
decides.

## Attestation Sign And Verify

Create a statement from a decision, sign it with a host signer, and attach it
as `_agent_trust`. Give each statement its own nonce: verification with
`consume: true` rejects a statement that has none.

```elixir
alias SigilGuard.Attestation

nonce = Base.encode16(:crypto.strong_rand_bytes(16), case: :lower)

{:ok, statement} =
  Attestation.from_decision(decision, context,
    payload: request,
    statement_type: :tool_request,
    nonce: nonce
  )

{:ok, envelope} =
  Attestation.sign(statement, MyApp.AgentSigner,
    keyid: "sha256:65b60673d6ed884bf01c2c222d82ada0740f29ac3355d6a925c81f17f47a27b8"
  )

request = Attestation.attach(request, envelope)
```

Verify an attached attestation with direct trust material or keys derived from a
verified trust bundle.

```elixir
trust_material = %{
  "sha256:65b60673d6ed884bf01c2c222d82ada0740f29ac3355d6a925c81f17f47a27b8" =>
    public_key_b64u
}

with {:ok, envelope} <- Attestation.fetch(request),
     {:ok, statement} <-
       Attestation.verify(envelope, trust_material,
         payload: request,
         context: context,
         consume: true
       ) do
  {:ok, statement}
end
```

`consume: true` records the nonce in `SigilGuard.ReplayStore`. A second
verification of the same envelope returns `{:error, :replay_detected}`, and a
statement signed without a nonce returns `{:error, :invalid_payload}`.

## Confirmation Flow

Confirmation is bound to the exact payload and boundary context. If either
changes, verification fails. `guard_request/3` and `guard_result/3` verify the
token themselves when they receive `confirmation_key:`.

```elixir
secret_key = System.fetch_env!("SIGILGUARD_CONFIRMATION_KEY")

decision = SigilGuard.ToolGateway.guard_request(request, context)

{:ok, token} =
  SigilGuard.ToolGateway.issue_confirmation(
    request,
    context,
    decision,
    secret_key,
    ttl_ms: 120_000
  )

confirmed_request = SigilGuard.Attestation.attach_confirmation(request, token)

case SigilGuard.ToolGateway.guard_request(
       confirmed_request,
       context,
       confirmation_key: secret_key
     ) do
  %SigilGuard.Decision{action: :allow} -> execute_tool(confirmed_request)
  %SigilGuard.Decision{} = denied -> reject_request(denied)
end
```

A token is consumed on first use. Use the out-of-band option when the host
keeps tokens outside the MCP payload:

```elixir
SigilGuard.ToolGateway.guard_request(
  request,
  context,
  confirmation_key: secret_key,
  confirmation_token: token
)
```

Result confirmation follows the same pattern: issue the token with
`direction: :result` and pass it to `guard_result/3`. A confirmed quarantine
comes back as `:redact` with the sanitized result, never the raw tool output.
`SigilGuard.MCP.Gateway` exposes the same token handling as
`guard_confirmed_request/3` and `guard_confirmed_result/3`; its
`guard_request/3` and `guard_result/3` ignore tokens.
